BUILD_ONLY: ghcr.io/mll-lab-nu/efe-private:ambench-<images/ambench/VERSION> is never pushed to a public registry
(docs/protocol/README.md §2.4, D5). Every AM-Bench task builds it itself, as its `ambench-base` stage (this Dockerfile without
its header, copied into the task's environment/Dockerfile and tests/Dockerfile): Harbor builds it on a machine's first
AM-Bench run from public sources only, and every later task reuses the layers. The organization keeps a prebuilt copy in
its private package efe-private, pushed with inline cache metadata; the tasks name it as compose `cache_from`, so a
runner that can read it pulls the layers instead of building them, and one that cannot gets a warning and builds.

Why
  The image contains NVIDIA Isaac Sim 5.1 / Omniverse Kit and the pip wheels of the Isaac stack (from pypi.nvidia.com).
  NVIDIA licenses them for use, not for redistribution, so the built image may not be published. Everything else in it may
  be: AM-Bench (github.com/ambench/ambench @ 60bf5b7, Apache-2.0, including its robot and object USDs; a few files derived
  from Isaac Lab stay BSD-3-Clause, THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md in /opt/ambench), Isaac Lab (github.com/isaac-sim/IsaacLab
  @ f4aa17f, BSD-3-Clause), Pyroki (AM-Bench's fork github.com/ambench/pyroki @ 2eafc99, MIT) and acados
  (github.com/acados/acados @ fa0af20, BSD-2-Clause, built with qpOASES).

What the builder accepts
  - the NVIDIA Isaac Sim / Omniverse license agreement (the image sets OMNI_KIT_ACCEPT_EULA=YES and ACCEPT_EULA=Y, and every
    container that runs it accepts it again at launch);
  - NVIDIA's terms for the isaacsim wheels on pypi.nvidia.com and for the assets of NVIDIA's asset bucket listed in
    images/ambench/web_assets.txt;
  - the licences above (Apache-2.0, BSD-3-Clause, MIT, BSD-2-Clause).
  No account, key or token is needed: everything is downloaded anonymously.

What is left out on purpose
  AM-Bench's scripted experts (source/ambench/ambench/policies/scripted/: they solve the tasks; the task registrations'
  pointer to them is blanked), its policy-learning package (ambench_learn), its scripts, documentation and git history.
  The build fails if anything left in /opt/ambench/source still names them. The reference solutions live in the task
  directories' solution/ only (recorded with the expert mounted from a checkout outside the image).

Data
  The web assets the scenes would otherwise load from NVIDIA's asset bucket at run time are baked in under /opt/isaac-assets,
  mirroring the bucket's layout (images/ambench/web_assets.txt, each checked against its sha256): Isaac Lab's default
  ground, the wall's material, the TossBall bin's geometry (Container_B06, a payload: without it the bin has no collision)
  with its materials, the button's material and the robot's paint. The tooling points Isaac's asset root at the copies and
  localize_assets.py rewrites the URLs in four of AM-Bench's USDs to them. A task container has no general network access
  (the agent reaches only the model APIs and the package registries, the verifiers nothing), and Isaac Sim starts and
  renders offline. The frozen task instances live in the task directories.

Host
  Linux x86_64, Docker with the NVIDIA Container Toolkit, an RTX-class NVIDIA GPU with a driver Isaac Sim 5.1 supports (Isaac
  renders with RTX even headless, and AM-Bench runs PhysX on the GPU). Checked on an RTX 4090 (AMD Ryzen 9 7950X, driver
  580.159). Build: ~60 GB of free disk during the build (the image is ~22.4 GB), network access to Docker Hub, snapshot.ubuntu.com,
  github.com, pypi.org, download.pytorch.org, pypi.nvidia.com and omniverse-content-production.s3*.amazonaws.com; about 12 min
  alone on that host, 24 min as a task's first build on a clean daemon beside other builds (2026-10-19; acados compiles). Run: a camera-free simulator (the default, and the grader's replays) takes ~3 GB of host RAM and ~3 GB of GPU
  memory; with the two onboard cameras on (Session(cameras=True), the verifier's filmed illustration) ~13 GB and ~6.5 GB
  (Isaac's initialisation fails when the GPU runs out of memory: start fewer at once on a shared GPU). Physics runs on the
  GPU, so the recorded state hashes are those of this GPU model; another GPU model may need its own reference run (Check a
  build). Camera rendering makes GPU PhysX timing-dependent: only camera-free runs reproduce the hashes
  (tasks/ambench/README.md, Determinism).

Build (alone; Harbor does this inside every task's build)
  docker build --target ambench-base -t ghcr.io/mll-lab-nu/efe-private:ambench-$(cat images/ambench/VERSION) images/ambench
  The build follows AM-Bench's scripts/setup/install.sh (torch 2.7.0+cu128, Isaac Sim 5.1.0, Isaac Lab at YAMLab's commit,
  Pyroki with JAX 0.4.28 on the CPU, acados with qpOASES, AM-Bench), then sets every Python package to
  images/ambench/requirements.lock (the reference build's `uv pip freeze`) without resolving and fails unless the environment
  is exactly the lock.

Check a build
  The frozen instances must rebuild to their recorded state hashes, and a fresh-process replay of the reference trajectory
  must succeed and end in its recorded hash. With a task directory at hand:
    t=tasks/ambench/ambench-press-button-fahexa-i00-privileged
    docker run --rm --gpus all --shm-size 8g --network none -v $PWD/$t:/t ghcr.io/mll-lab-nu/efe-private:ambench-$(cat images/ambench/VERSION) \
      bash -c "cd /tmp && python /t/environment/rcb_ambench.py run-actions --instance /t/environment/instance.json \
                 --actions /t/solution/oracle_actions.npy --out /tmp/t.npz && \
               python /t/environment/rcb_ambench.py replay --instance /t/environment/instance.json --traj /tmp/t.npz"
  expects instance_matches true, success true and, from the replay, the end state_sha of the task README's Verification
  section (the oracle's hash; both commands run camera-free). Or run `harbor run -p tasks/ambench/ambench-press-button-fahexa-i00-privileged -a oracle`.

Publishing the cache copy (maintainers, after a change here and a VERSION bump)
  Build it as above with --build-arg BUILDKIT_INLINE_CACHE=1, check it (above), push it to
  ghcr.io/mll-lab-nu/efe-private:ambench-$(cat images/ambench/VERSION) (organization members only, never a public package),
  and copy the new stage into every task (with the new tag in their compose `cache_from`).
